# Batch 29 (2026-11-16): deep time and the Cradle of Humankind (oxygen, stromatolites, Tiktaalik, Mitochondrial Eve, Klasies, Diepkloof, Mrs Ples, Makapansgat pebble, Klerksdorp spheres);
# Assyria, Judah and the Persian Wars (Nineveh, Sennacherib, Siloam tunnel, Lilith, Marathon, Salamis) with Japan and China (Genji, Grand Canal, tengu);
# sacred rocks, treaties, eclipses and world trees (Naha Stone, Uluru, Waitangi, Isandlwana, Hero Twins, Yggdrasil, Adamastor);
# modern puzzles (Max Headroom hijacking, Kryptos, Grusch hearing, Calvine photo, red sprites, Mpemba effect, sailing stones)
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: deep time, early life and the Cradle of Humankind
E+=[
X('great-oxidation-event','The Great Oxidation Event: when microbes filled the air with oxygen',H,'confirmed',2,-2460000000,['geochemical','isotopic','geological'],
 "Between about 2.46 and 2.06 billion years ago, free oxygen first built up in Earth's atmosphere and shallow seas. Geologists infer that it came from cyanobacteria, microbes that had evolved photosynthesis releasing oxygen as a by-product. For a long time the oxygen was soaked up by dissolved iron, laying down the banded iron formations that supply most of today's iron ore. Once those 'sinks' were used up, oxygen accumulated. Because oxygen is toxic to many organisms that evolved without it, the event is sometimes called the Oxygen Catastrophe, and it may have contributed to the Huronian glaciations. It also paved the way for oxygen-breathing cells and, much later, complex life.",
 [('Debate over timing and cause','Researchers still argue over why oxygen rose when it did. Proposals include a slow decline in volcanic gases that consumed oxygen, changes in nutrient supply, and the gradual lengthening of Earth\'s day, which gave microbial mats more continuous daylight. Some evidence hints at short "whiffs" of oxygen before the main event.'),
  ('Life reshaping a planet','The event is often cited as the first time living things changed the chemistry of the whole planet. Some writers compare it to human-caused change today; scientists caution that the scales of time are very different.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Oxidation_Event','doi:10.1038/nature13068','wiki:Banded_iron_formation'],img='File:Black-band ironstone (aka).jpg',end=-2060000000,dateNote='c. 2.46–2.06 billion years ago'),
X('early-stromatolites','Stromatolites: the oldest signs of life, and the 3.7-billion-year argument',H,'disputed',2,-3700000000,['palaeontological','geochemical','geological'],
 "Stromatolites are layered rock mounds built by mats of microbes, such as cyanobacteria, that trap and cement sediment layer by layer. Fossil stromatolites are among the oldest accepted evidence of life. Those in the Pilbara region of Western Australia, about 3.48 to 3.43 billion years old, are widely accepted, and a 2006 study described a 3.43-billion-year-old stromatolite reef in the Strelley Pool Formation. Living stromatolites still grow in a few places, most famously Shark Bay in Western Australia. They give a rare glimpse of what the shallow seas of the early Earth looked like.",
 [('Life at 3.7 billion years?','In 2016 a team reported cone-shaped structures in 3.7-billion-year-old rocks at Isua, Greenland, as the oldest stromatolites. A 2018 study of the same outcrop argued that the structures were deformed rock layers, not biological. The question is still debated.'),
  ('Look-alikes','Some scientists warn that chemical and physical processes can form layered mounds without life, so a stromatolite shape alone is not proof. This is why researchers look for chemical traces of microbes as well.')],
 ['wiki:Stromatolite','doi:10.1038/nature04764','doi:10.1038/nature19355','doi:10.1038/s41586-018-0610-4'],img='Stromatolite',end=-3430000000,dateNote='Pilbara stromatolites c. 3.48–3.43 billion years ago; disputed Isua structures c. 3.7 billion years ago',
 changed='2006: Strelley Pool reef described. 2016: Isua structures announced as older stromatolites. 2018: reanalysis argued they are not biological.'),
X('tiktaalik','Tiktaalik: the "fishapod" found where scientists predicted',H,'confirmed',2,-375000000,['fossil','geological dating'],
 "Tiktaalik roseae was a lobe-finned fish that lived about 375 million years ago, in the late Devonian. It had gills and scales, but also a flat, crocodile-like head, a neck, and sturdy fins with wrist-like bones that could prop it up in shallow water. Its fossils were found in 2004 on Ellesmere Island in Arctic Canada by a team including Neil Shubin, Edward Daeschler and Farish Jenkins, and described in Nature in 2006. The team chose the site because rocks of the right age and type, laid down in shallow streams, were exposed there. Tiktaalik is often presented as a transitional form between fish and four-legged animals (tetrapods). Its name means 'large freshwater fish' in Inuktitut, chosen with the advice of Inuit elders.",
 [('Older footprints','In 2010 researchers described tetrapod-like trackways from Zachełmie, Poland, dated to about 395 million years ago, older than Tiktaalik. This suggests that four-legged animals already existed earlier, so Tiktaalik may be a late survivor of a transitional group rather than a direct ancestor.'),
  ('Creationist objections','Some creationist writers argue that Tiktaalik is simply an unusual fish and not evidence of evolution, and point to the Polish tracks. Palaeontologists reply that a transitional form does not need to be a direct ancestor to show how the change from fins to limbs happened.')],
 ['wiki:Tiktaalik','doi:10.1038/nature04639','doi:10.1038/nature08623'],img='Tiktaalik',changed='2004: fossils found; 2006: described in Nature; 2010: older Zachełmie trackways complicated the picture.'),
X('mitochondrial-eve','Mitochondrial Eve and Y-chromosomal Adam: the shared ancestors in our DNA',H,'confirmed',2,-155000,['genetic'],
 "Mitochondrial DNA passes almost only from mother to child. By comparing it across living people, geneticists can trace all maternal lines back to one woman, the most recent common ancestor of everyone alive today along the mother's line. She has been nicknamed 'Mitochondrial Eve'. A 1987 study in Nature by Rebecca Cann, Mark Stoneking and Allan Wilson placed her in Africa about 200,000 years ago. As of 2013, estimates were around 155,000 years. Her male counterpart, 'Y-chromosomal Adam', is traced through the Y chromosome passed from father to son; estimates as of 2015 put him around 200,000 to 300,000 years ago. The two need not have lived at the same time or met.",
 [('Not the first woman','Geneticists stress that she was not the only woman alive, nor the first human. Many of her contemporaries also have descendants today, just not through an unbroken line of mothers. The title also moves forward in time whenever a maternal line dies out.'),
  ('Echo of Eden','The biblical nickname has led some religious writers to connect the finding to Adam and Eve. Scientists note that the genetic \'Eve\' and \'Adam\' are separated by tens of thousands of years and lived among large populations, which differs from the Genesis account.')],
 ['wiki:Mitochondrial_Eve','doi:10.1038/325031a0','wiki:Y-chromosomal_Adam'],img='Mitochondrial Eve',dateNote='Estimates vary; c. 155,000 years ago (as of 2013)'),
X('klasies-river','Klasies River Caves: early Homo sapiens on the Cape coast',H,'confirmed',3,-125000,['archaeological','fossil'],
 "The Klasies River Caves lie in a sandstone cliff on the Tsitsikamma coast of South Africa's Eastern Cape. Excavations by Ronald Singer and John Wymer in 1967–1968, and later by Hilary Deacon and Sarah Wurz, revealed Middle Stone Age occupation beginning about 125,000 years ago. More than 50 human fossils, mostly fragments, belong to early Homo sapiens. The layers also contain stone tools, shellfish, burnt food remains, and from the Howiesons Poort period (about 80,000–65,000 years ago) a ground bone point and a charred bone with engraved lines. The site is a key record of how modern human behaviour developed in southern Africa.",
 [('Cannibalism?','Cut marks and burning on some human bones have led some researchers to propose episodic cannibalism. Others say the evidence is too limited to know whether this happened, or whether the marks come from funeral practices.'),
  ('How "modern" were they?','Scholars debate whether the people of Klasies were fully modern in behaviour or whether such behaviour emerged gradually. The site is often compared with Blombos Cave and Pinnacle Point.')],
 ['wiki:Klasies_River_Caves'],img=None,end=-55000,dateNote='Occupation from c. 125,000 years ago'),
X('diepkloof-eggshells','Diepkloof: engraved ostrich-egg water flasks, 60,000 years ago',H,'confirmed',3,-60000,['archaeological','dating'],
 "At Diepkloof Rock Shelter, near Elands Bay about 150 km north of Cape Town, archaeologists found hundreds of fragments of ostrich eggshell engraved with repeated geometric patterns, such as hatched bands and parallel lines. Some had holes at one end, like ostrich-egg water containers still used by San people in recent times. A 2010 study dated them to about 60,000 years ago, in the Howiesons Poort period. The researchers argued that the repeated motifs were a shared graphic tradition, possibly marking the identity of a person or group. In 2024 the site became part of the World Heritage Site 'Pleistocene Occupation Sites of South Africa'.",
 [('Symbols or decoration?','Most specialists accept the patterns as deliberate and symbolic. Some argue it is hard to know what they meant: they may be personal marks, group signs, or simply decoration.'),
  ('One of many beginnings','The find adds to evidence from Blombos and other southern African sites that symbolic behaviour was present in Africa long before cave art appeared in Europe.')],
 ['wiki:Diepkloof_Rock_Shelter','doi:10.1073/pnas.0913047107'],img='Diepkloof Rock Shelter'),
X('mrs-ples','Mrs Ples: the skull blasted out with dynamite',H,'confirmed',2,-3700000,['fossil','geological dating'],
 "On 18 April 1947 Robert Broom and John T. Robinson found the most complete skull of Australopithecus africanus ever recovered in South Africa, at Sterkfontein, about 40 km northwest of Johannesburg. Broom used dynamite and pickaxes, so the skull came out in pieces and some fragments are missing. Broom first named it Plesianthropus transvaalensis ('near-man from the Transvaal'), and his young co-workers nicknamed it 'Mrs Ples'. Scientists call it STS 5. It helped persuade sceptics that Raymond Dart's Taung Child was a genuine human relative. It is kept at the Ditsong National Museum of Natural History in Pretoria, and the area is now part of the Cradle of Humankind World Heritage Site.",
 [('Mrs or Mr?','Some researchers have argued from the skull\'s features that it may be male, but the question has not been settled and the nickname has stayed.'),
  ('How old?','The skull was first dated to about 2.1–2.6 million years ago. A 2022 study using cosmogenic nuclide dating of the surrounding rocks gave 3.4–3.7 million years, which would make it as old as Lucy. Other researchers question whether the new dates apply to the fossils themselves.')],
 ['wiki:Mrs._Ples','doi:10.1073/pnas.2123516119'],img='Mrs. Ples',end=-2100000,dateNote='First dated c. 2.1–2.6 million years; 2022 study: c. 3.4–3.7 million years (debated); found 1947',
 changed='1947: found; dated to c. 2.1–2.6 million years; 2022: cosmogenic dating of the cave rocks suggested 3.4–3.7 million years.'),
X('makapansgat-pebble','The Makapansgat pebble: a 3-million-year-old stone face',H,'disputed',3,-3000000,['archaeological','geological'],
 "In 1925 a schoolteacher, Wilfred Eitzman, found a reddish-brown jasper pebble in a cave in the Makapan Valley, north of Mokopane, South Africa. Natural chipping and weathering give it what look like two eyes and a mouth, so it resembles a face. The cave also held fossils of australopithecines. The pebble was found several kilometres from the nearest possible natural source of this kind of stone, so some scholars suggest an early hominin noticed the 'face' and carried it back about 3 million years ago. No traces of artificial shaping have been found; the markings are natural. It is held at the University of the Witwatersrand in Johannesburg.",
 [('The oldest manuport','Some researchers, such as Robert Bednarik, argue that the pebble is the oldest known manuport (an object carried by a human relative) and possibly the earliest sign of a sense of beauty or of seeing faces in things.'),
  ('Carried by nature','Sceptics say the pebble could have reached the cave by water, animals or other natural means, and that there is no way to prove a hominin chose it.')],
 ['wiki:Makapansgat_pebble'],img='Makapansgat pebble',dateNote='c. 3 million years ago; found 1925'),
X('klerksdorp-spheres','Klerksdorp spheres: "ancient machined balls" in 3-billion-year-old rock',H,'debunked',3,1982,['geological','popular claims'],
 "Miners at the Wonderstone pyrophyllite quarries near Ottosdal, South Africa, collect small spheres and discs, usually 0.5–10 cm across, from rocks about 3 billion years old. In popular books, magazine articles (including a 1982 issue of Scope) and websites they have been described as manufactured objects, some with grooves around the middle, made by an unknown ancient civilisation or by aliens. Geologists who have cut them open, such as Paul Heinrich, found radiating crystal structures of hematite or wollastonite. They concluded that the spheres are natural concretions that grew inside volcanic sediments as the rock changed over time.",
 [('Out-of-place artefacts','Writers on ancient mysteries have cited the spheres as evidence of intelligent makers billions of years ago, pointing to their regular shapes and grooves. These claims are speculative and have no support in geological studies.'),
  ('The spinning-sphere story','Popular articles quoted Roelf Marx, a former curator of the Klerksdorp Museum, as saying a sphere rotated by itself in a sealed display case. Marx later said he had been misquoted: it moved because of earth tremors from blasting in nearby gold mines.')],
 ['wiki:Klerksdorp_sphere'],img='Klerksdorp sphere',dateNote='rocks c. 3 billion years old; popularised from the 1980s'),
]
# --- part 2: Assyria, Judah, the Persian Wars; Japan and China
E+=[
X('fall-of-nineveh','The fall of Nineveh, 612 BCE: the end of Assyria and the legend of Sardanapalus',H,'confirmed',2,-612,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 612 BCE an alliance of Medes and Babylonians besieged and captured Nineveh, near modern Mosul in Iraq. It was the capital of the Neo-Assyrian Empire, then among the greatest cities in the world. The Babylonian chronicle known as the Fall of Nineveh Chronicle records the siege and the city's capture. Within three years the Assyrian Empire was gone, and Babylon became the centre of a new empire. Archaeology shows that Nineveh was largely abandoned afterwards, and its palaces, including the library of Ashurbanipal, lay buried until excavations in the 1840s and 1850s. The Hebrew prophet Nahum celebrated its fall.",
 [('Sardanapalus','A garbled Greek account turned the fall into the legend of Sardanapalus, a decadent last king who burned himself, his treasure and his concubines on a pyre. Historians see the story as a mix of several real kings and moral storytelling.'),
  ('Flood or fire?','Some ancient writers said the river rose and broke the walls. Archaeologists found burned palaces and destruction layers; whether flooding also helped the attackers is not settled.')],
 ['wiki:Fall_of_Nineveh','wiki:Nineveh','wiki:Sardanapalus'],img='Fall of Nineveh'),
X('sennacherib-judah-701','Sennacherib against Judah, 701 BCE: the Lachish reliefs and the angel of death',H,'confirmed',2,-701,['textual','archaeological','relief sculpture'],
 "In 701 BCE the Assyrian king Sennacherib marched west to crush a rebellion. He captured Lachish, the second city of the Kingdom of Judah, and had the siege carved on stone reliefs in his palace at Nineveh, now in the British Museum. Assyrian records, such as Sennacherib's annals on clay prisms, say he shut up King Hezekiah in Jerusalem 'like a bird in a cage' and received heavy tribute, but they do not claim the city was taken. Hezekiah stayed on the throne as a tribute-paying vassal. Excavations at Lachish found a siege ramp, arrowheads and destruction layers.",
 [('The angel of the Lord','The Hebrew Bible (2 Kings 18–19, Isaiah 36–37) says an angel struck down 185,000 Assyrian soldiers in one night outside Jerusalem, and Sennacherib went home. Believers read this as a miracle; many historians think it reflects a real withdrawal, perhaps because of disease or the tribute paid.'),
  ('The mice of Herodotus','The Greek historian Herodotus told an Egyptian version: field mice gnawed the Assyrians\' bowstrings and shields, forcing them to retreat. Some scholars link mice to plague, but this is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Sennacherib%27s_campaign_in_the_Levant','wiki:Lachish_reliefs','wiki:Siege_of_Lachish'],img='Siege of Lachish'),
X('siloam-tunnel','Hezekiah\'s tunnel: two teams digging blind through the rock',H,'confirmed',3,-700,['archaeological','epigraphic','radiometric dating'],
 "The Siloam tunnel is a water channel about 533 m long, cut through the rock under the City of David in Jerusalem to carry water from the Gihon Spring to the Pool of Siloam. The Bible says King Hezekiah redirected the Gihon waters to protect Jerusalem from an Assyrian siege (2 Kings 20; 2 Chronicles 32:30). In 1880 an inscription was found inside the tunnel. In early Hebrew it describes how two teams of diggers, working from both ends, heard each other's voices through the rock and met in the middle. A 2003 study using radiocarbon and uranium-thorium dating supported a date around 700 BCE. The inscription is now in the Istanbul Archaeology Museums.",
 [('Earlier than Hezekiah?','Excavations published in 2011 suggested that the tunnel might have been started in the late 9th or early 8th century BCE, before Hezekiah.'),
  ('How did they meet?','The tunnel winds in an S shape. Engineers and archaeologists debate whether the diggers followed a natural crack in the rock, used sound, or had another method.')],
 ['wiki:Siloam_tunnel','wiki:Siloam_inscription','doi:10.1038/nature01909'],img='Siloam tunnel',dateNote='c. 700 BCE (some argue earlier)'),
X('lilith','Lilith: from night demon to Adam\'s first wife',M,'myth-legend',2,800,['textual','folklore','incantation bowls'],
 "Lilith is a female figure in Jewish folklore. Her roots are often traced to Mesopotamian demons called lilitu, linked to wind and night. The Hebrew Bible mentions a 'lilith' once (Isaiah 34:14), among wild creatures living in ruins. Later Aramaic incantation bowls and the Babylonian Talmud describe Liliths as dangerous night spirits. The best-known story comes from the Alphabet of Ben Sira, a satirical medieval text (c. 700–1000 CE): Lilith was created from the earth like Adam, refused to be beneath him, and flew away; she was then said to threaten newborn babies, and amulets protected children from her.",
 [('A feminist symbol','From the 1970s some feminist writers adopted Lilith as a symbol of independence, reading her as a woman punished for demanding equality. Others note that the original sources present her as a demon.'),
  ('The Burney Relief','A Babylonian clay plaque of a winged woman with bird talons (c. 1800 BCE, British Museum) is sometimes called the \'Queen of the Night\' and was once linked to Lilith. Most scholars now think it shows a goddess such as Ishtar or Ereshkigal.')],
 ['wiki:Lilith','wiki:Alphabet_of_Ben_Sira','wiki:Burney_Relief'],img='Lilith',dateNote='Alphabet of Ben Sira c. 700–1000 CE; older Mesopotamian roots'),
X('battle-of-marathon','Marathon, 490 BCE: the battle, the god Pan, and the run that may never have happened',H,'confirmed',1,-490,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 490 BCE an Athenian army, helped by Plataea, defeated a Persian force sent by King Darius I on the plain of Marathon, northeast of Athens. Herodotus, writing a few decades later, says that 192 Athenians died and about 6,400 Persians. The burial mound of the Athenian dead, the Soros, can still be seen. Herodotus also says that before the battle the runner Pheidippides ran from Athens to Sparta, about 240 km, to ask for help, and that the god Pan met him on the way and asked why the Athenians did not honour him. The Spartans arrived only after the battle, because they were celebrating a religious festival.",
 [('The first marathon','The story of a runner who ran from Marathon to Athens, announced victory and died appears centuries later, in Plutarch (1st century CE) and Lucian. Herodotus does not mention it. The tale inspired the marathon race at the 1896 Olympic Games. Historians consider it a legend that may mix up the run to Sparta.'),
  ('Pan and the "panic"','The Athenians later built a shrine to Pan. Some writers link Pan to the sudden fear ("panic") that was said to strike enemies, though this link is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Marathon','wiki:Pheidippides'],img='Battle of Marathon'),
X('battle-of-salamis','Salamis, 480 BCE: the sea battle that saved Athens',H,'confirmed',2,-480,['textual'],
 "In September 480 BCE, after the Persian king Xerxes had won at Thermopylae and burned Athens, a Greek fleet led by the Athenian Themistocles trapped the much larger Persian fleet in the narrow strait between the island of Salamis and the mainland. In the cramped water the Persian ships could not use their numbers, and many were sunk. Xerxes is said to have watched from a throne on the shore. He returned to Asia with much of his army, and the Greeks won the war the next year at Plataea. The playwright Aeschylus, who may have fought at Salamis, staged 'The Persians' about it in 472 BCE, the oldest surviving Greek play.",
 [('Trick or treachery?','Herodotus says Themistocles sent a slave to tell Xerxes that the Greeks were about to flee, luring the Persians into the strait. Some historians doubt the story or think it was told later to make Themistocles look clever.'),
  ('Did it save the West?','Many historians have called Salamis a turning point for Greek and Western civilisation. Others see this as exaggerated and point out that Persia remained a great power for 150 years.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Salamis','wiki:The_Persians'],img='Battle of Salamis'),
X('tale-of-genji','The Tale of Genji: a court lady writes one of the first novels',H,'confirmed',2,1008,['textual'],
 "The Tale of Genji was written in the early 11th century by Murasaki Shikibu, a lady-in-waiting at the Japanese imperial court in Heian-kyō (Kyoto). Its 54 chapters follow the life and loves of Hikaru Genji, the 'shining' son of an emperor, and then his descendants. It is written in Japanese using kana script, and is famous for its psychology, poetry and picture of court life. Murasaki's own diary mentions the tale around 1008. No manuscript from her lifetime survives; the oldest copies and the famous illustrated handscrolls date from the 12th century onwards.",
 [('The first novel?','The tale is often called the world\'s first novel. Some scholars prefer \'first psychological novel\' or reject the label, pointing to earlier long prose works, such as Greek and Latin novels.'),
  ('One author or several?','Some scholars have suggested that the final chapters (the \'Uji chapters\') may have been written or reworked by someone else. Most still attribute the whole work to Murasaki Shikibu.')],
 ['wiki:The_Tale_of_Genji','wiki:Murasaki_Shikibu'],img='The Tale of Genji',dateNote='Early 11th century; mentioned in 1008'),
X('grand-canal-china','The Grand Canal of China: the longest artificial river',H,'confirmed',2,605,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Grand Canal is a system of waterways linking Beijing in the north with Hangzhou in the south. Its main route today is about 1,776 km long, the longest canal in the world. The oldest sections were dug in the 5th century BCE. Under Emperor Yang of the Sui dynasty, in the early 600s CE, earlier canals were linked into a single network joining the Yellow River and the Yangtze. This let grain from the rich south feed the capital and armies in the north. The work used huge forced labour, and Sui rule soon collapsed. Later dynasties, especially the Yuan, rebuilt and extended it. UNESCO listed it as a World Heritage Site in 2014.",
 [('A cause of the Sui collapse','Traditional Chinese histories blamed the Sui fall on the cost of the canal and Emperor Yang\'s wars and luxury. Some modern historians think this picture was exaggerated by the next dynasty, the Tang.'),
  ('Water and power','Some scholars see the canal as an example of how controlling water helped build and hold together large states; others think this idea, sometimes called "hydraulic despotism", is too simple.')],
 ['wiki:Grand_Canal_(China)','https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1443|UNESCO World Heritage: The Grand Canal|heritage'],img='Grand Canal (China)',dateNote='Oldest sections 5th century BCE; Sui network early 600s CE'),
X('tengu','Tengu: the mountain goblins that began as a roaring shooting star',M,'myth-legend',2,637,['textual','folklore','art'],
 "Tengu are supernatural beings of Japanese folklore, linked to mountains and forests. The Nihon Shoki, written in 720, is generally held to contain their first recorded mention: in an entry for 637, a large shooting star with a thunderous sound was named a tengu by a Buddhist monk, with a reading glossed as amatsukitsune, 'heavenly fox'. Early tengu were bird-like, kite-shaped demons; later art shows a red-faced figure with a very long nose, dressed like the yamabushi mountain ascetics. Buddhism long treated tengu as disruptive demons and omens of war, but over time they became dangerous protectors of mountains and teachers of swordsmanship.",
 [('Origins in China and India','The name uses the Chinese characters for tiangou, the \'heavenly dog\' that was linked to meteors and eclipses in China. Some scholars also see influence from the Indian bird-being Garuda.'),
  ('Real encounters','Japanese folklore includes many stories of people, especially children, taken by tengu and later returned. Folklorists compare these to fairy abductions in Europe.')],
 ['wiki:Tengu'],img='File:Tengu Kuniyoshi.jpg',dateNote='First recorded mention: Nihon Shoki (720), entry for 637'),
]
# --- part 3: sacred rocks, treaties, eclipses, world trees
E+=[
X('naha-stone','The Naha Stone: lift it and rule all Hawaii',M,'myth-legend',3,1772,['oral tradition','physical object'],
 "The Naha Stone is a volcanic boulder weighing about 2.5–3.5 tonnes, now outside the Hilo Public Library on the Island of Hawaii, where it was moved in 1952. Tradition says it came from Kauaʻi by double canoe. It was used to test babies said to be of the Naha royal line: a child placed on it who stayed silent was accepted. A legend said that whoever could overturn the stone would unite all the Hawaiian islands. Kamehameha, who did go on to unify Hawaii by 1810, is said to have moved it as a young man.",
 [('A prophecy fulfilled','Hawaiian tradition presents Kamehameha\'s feat as a sign that he was destined to rule, and it is a well-known part of his story.'),
  ('Legend after the fact','Historians note that stories like this are often told after a ruler succeeds, to support his right to rule. There is no independent record of the event; accounts differ on his age.')],
 ['wiki:Naha_Stone','wiki:Kamehameha_I'],img='Naha Stone',dateNote='Kamehameha\'s feat traditionally placed in his youth (18th century); exact date unknown'),
X('uluru','Uluru: the sacred rock and the end of the climb',M,'myth-legend',2,1985,['oral tradition','geological','historical records'],
 "Uluru is a huge sandstone monolith in the Northern Territory of Australia, rising about 348 m above the plain. Geologists say it formed from sediments laid down over 500 million years ago and later tilted and exposed by erosion. For the Aṉangu, its traditional owners, Uluru is a place of Tjukurpa (often translated 'Dreaming'), with sites that record the deeds of ancestral beings such as the python woman Kuniya and the venomous snake man Liru. On 26 October 1985 the Australian government returned ownership to the Aṉangu, who leased it back as a national park. After years of requests from the Aṉangu, the climb was closed on 26 October 2019 and the chain handhold was removed.",
 [('Ancient presence','Archaeological evidence shows that Aboriginal people have lived in the area for many thousands of years. Aṉangu tradition holds that they have always been there.'),
  ('Curse of the stolen rocks','Park staff have received many rocks that tourists took and later mailed back, often with letters blaming them for bad luck. This is a modern legend; the Aṉangu do not describe it as part of their law.')],
 ['wiki:Uluru','https://parksaustralia.gov.au/uluru/|Parks Australia: Uluru-Kata Tjuta National Park|official'],img='Uluru',dateNote='Handback 1985; climb closed 2019'),
X('treaty-of-waitangi','The Treaty of Waitangi: one treaty, two texts',H,'confirmed',2,1840,['textual','historical records'],
 "On 6 February 1840 representatives of the British Crown and more than 40 Māori chiefs signed a treaty at Waitangi in New Zealand; copies were later signed by about 500 chiefs in total. It became the founding document of New Zealand. There are English and Māori versions, and they differ. In the English text the chiefs cede 'all the rights and powers of sovereignty'. In the Māori text they grant 'kawanatanga' (governance) while keeping 'tino rangatiratanga' (full chieftainship) over their lands, villages and 'taonga' (treasures). Land sales and wars followed, and many Māori lost their lands. Since 1975 the Waitangi Tribunal has investigated breaches.",
 [('Which text counts?','Many academics and the Waitangi Tribunal give extra weight to the Māori text, which most chiefs signed, and argue that Māori never gave up sovereignty. Others hold that the English text and later law established Crown sovereignty.'),
  ('A living debate','The meaning of the treaty is still debated in New Zealand politics, including recent arguments over how its principles should be written into law.')],
 ['wiki:Treaty_of_Waitangi','https://nzhistory.govt.nz/politics/treaty-of-waitangi|NZHistory: Treaty of Waitangi|government'],img='Treaty of Waitangi'),
X('isandlwana-1879','Isandlwana, 1879: a Zulu victory under a darkening sun',H,'confirmed',2,1879,['historical records','oral tradition','astronomical'],
 "On 22 January 1879, early in the Anglo-Zulu War, a Zulu army of about 20,000 attacked a British camp at the foot of Isandlwana hill in Zululand. The British force was largely destroyed: more than 1,300 British soldiers and African auxiliaries were killed, one of the worst defeats of a modern army by an indigenous force. The Zulu also lost heavily. That afternoon, around 2:30 pm, a partial solar eclipse darkened the sky over the battlefield. Britain sent reinforcements and defeated the Zulu kingdom later in 1879, but the battle changed British tactics and is still remembered in South Africa.",
 [('An omen in the sky','Some accounts, including oral memories gathered later, connect the darkened sun with the slaughter. Historians treat these as later interpretations; the eclipse was real but partial.'),
  ('Who was to blame?','British officials blamed Colonel Anthony Durnford, who died in the battle. Later historians argue that the commander Lord Chelmsford\'s decisions, such as splitting his force, were more to blame.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Isandlwana','wiki:Anglo-Zulu_War'],img='Battle of Isandlwana'),
X('hero-twins','The Maya Hero Twins: ballgame against the Lords of Death',M,'myth-legend',2,200,['textual','art','archaeological'],
 "The Hero Twins, Hunahpu and Xbalanque, are the central figures of the Popol Vuh, a Kʼicheʼ Maya text written down in the 16th century, which preserves the oldest complete Maya myth. Their father and uncle were summoned to Xibalba, the underworld, by the Lords of Death, lost a ballgame and were killed. The father's head, hung in a tree, made a daughter of an underworld lord pregnant, and she gave birth to the twins. They grew up to defeat their monstrous enemies, survived the trials of Xibalba, beat the Lords at the ballgame and through trickery, and finally rose into the sky, often identified as the Sun and the Moon. The twins have also been identified in the art of the Classic Maya (200–900 CE), many centuries before the Popol Vuh was written down.",
 [('Myth and kingship','Classic Maya kings often linked themselves to the Hero Twins and other mythic figures. Some scholars think the ballgame re-enacted the twins\' victory over death.'),
  ('Twins worldwide','Divine twins appear in many mythologies, such as Romulus and Remus, the Greek Dioscuri and the Vedic Ashvins. Some see a deep shared pattern; others see independent inventions arising from the fascination with twins.')],
 ['wiki:Maya_Hero_Twins','wiki:Popol_Vuh'],img='Maya Hero Twins',dateNote='Identified in Classic Maya art (200–900 CE); written down in the 16th century'),
X('yggdrasil','Yggdrasil: the World Tree of Norse myth',M,'myth-legend',2,1220,['textual'],
 "In Norse mythology Yggdrasil is an immense ash tree at the centre of the cosmos, whose branches and roots connect the worlds of gods, giants, humans and the dead. It is described in the Poetic Edda, a collection of poems preserved in a 13th-century Icelandic manuscript, and in the Prose Edda written by Snorri Sturluson around 1220. Three roots reach to wells or springs; the Norns, who shape fate, tend it. A dragon, Níðhöggr, gnaws its roots, an eagle sits at the top, and a squirrel, Ratatoskr, runs between them carrying insults. The god Odin hung for nine nights on a tree, usually identified as Yggdrasil, to gain the runes. At Ragnarök the tree shakes but survives.",
 [('A shared pattern','World trees connecting heaven, earth and underworld appear in many cultures, from Siberian shamanism to the Maya ceiling tree. Some scholars see a very old shared idea; others think the image arises independently because trees link the ground and the sky.'),
  ('Christian influence?','Because the Eddas were written down by Christians, some researchers think the image of Odin on the tree may have been shaped by the crucifixion; others argue it is an older pagan theme.')],
 ['wiki:Yggdrasil','wiki:Poetic_Edda','wiki:Prose_Edda'],img='File:Yggdrasil.jpg',dateNote='Written sources 13th century (Prose Edda c. 1220); older oral tradition'),
X('adamastor','Adamastor: the giant of the Cape of Storms',M,'myth-legend',2,1572,['literary','historical records'],
 "In 1488 the Portuguese navigator Bartolomeu Dias sailed round the southern tip of Africa; according to tradition he named it the Cape of Storms, and King John II renamed it the Cape of Good Hope. In 1572 the poet Luís de Camões published Os Lusíadas, an epic about Vasco da Gama's voyage to India. In it, a monstrous giant named Adamastor rises from the sea at the Cape, warns of disasters that will strike later Portuguese ships, and tells how the gods turned him into the rocky headland as punishment for loving a sea nymph. Adamastor became a lasting symbol of the Cape in poetry, from Fernando Pessoa to André Brink.",
 [('Myth from real danger','Many readers see Adamastor as a personification of the storms, currents and shipwrecks that made the Cape so dangerous for sailors. Other Cape legends, such as the Flying Dutchman, grew from the same fears.'),
  ('A colonial symbol','Some South African writers read Adamastor as a symbol of Africa resisting European arrival, while others see the poem as celebrating Portuguese expansion.')],
 ['wiki:Adamastor','wiki:Os_Lus%C3%ADadas','wiki:Bartolomeu_Dias'],img='Adamastor',dateNote='Cape rounded 1488; Os Lusíadas published 1572'),
]
# --- part 4: modern puzzles
E+=[
X('max-headroom-1987','The Max Headroom hijacking: Chicago\'s unsolved TV takeover',H,'confirmed',3,1987,['broadcast recordings','news reports'],
 "On the evening of 22 November 1987, two Chicago television stations were hijacked about two hours apart. During WGN-TV's evening news, a figure in a Max Headroom mask swayed in front of a moving metal panel for about 17 seconds, with only a buzzing sound, before engineers regained control. Later that night, during PBS station WTTW's broadcast of the Doctor Who serial Horror of Fang Rock, the masked figure returned for about 90 seconds with garbled audio, ending with the figure's bare buttocks being swatted by a woman with a flyswatter. The Federal Communications Commission investigated, but the people responsible have never been identified.",
 [('Insider job','Many investigators think the hijackers needed technical knowledge and equipment to overpower the stations\' microwave links, perhaps people with experience in broadcasting. Various online sleuths have named suspects, but none has been confirmed.'),
  ('Something stranger','The incident has become an internet legend, sometimes treated as creepy or mysterious. There is no evidence that it was anything more than a prank by unknown people.')],
 ['wiki:Max_Headroom_signal_hijackings'],img=None),
X('kryptos','Kryptos: the CIA\'s unsolved sculpture',H,'confirmed',3,1990,['physical object','cryptanalysis'],
 "Kryptos is a sculpture by the American artist Jim Sanborn at CIA headquarters in Langley, Virginia, dedicated on 3 November 1990. A curved copper screen bears four encrypted messages cut into the metal. The first three were solved by the late 1990s by a CIA analyst, by an NSA team and by a computer scientist, Jim Gillogly. The fourth, K4, became one of the world's most famous unsolved codes; Sanborn released clues, including the words BERLIN and CLOCK. In September 2025 two journalists, Jarett Kobek and Richard Byrne, said they had found what appeared to be the K4 plaintext among Sanborn's papers at the Smithsonian's Archives of American Art. Sanborn's K4 archive was auctioned in November 2025 for $962,500.",
 [('Still unsolved?','Some puzzle fans say that the archive find does not count as \'solving\' K4, since nobody has shown how to decrypt it from the sculpture. The Smithsonian sealed the files at Sanborn\'s request.'),
  ('A fifth message','Sanborn has said that a fifth message, K5, will become apparent once K4 is solved, which keeps the puzzle alive.')],
 ['wiki:Kryptos'],img='Kryptos',changed='1990: dedicated; 1999: K1–K3 solution announced publicly; 2025: K4 plaintext found in Smithsonian archive and the solution archive auctioned.'),
X('grusch-hearing-2023','The 2023 UFO hearing: David Grusch and "non-human biologics"',H,'disputed',2,2023,['testimony','official statements'],
 "On 26 July 2023, David Grusch, a former US Air Force intelligence officer who had served on the Unidentified Aerial Phenomena Task Force, testified under oath before a subcommittee of the US House Oversight Committee, along with two former Navy pilots. He said the US government had a long-running secret programme to recover and reverse-engineer craft of non-human origin, and that 'non-human biologics' had been recovered. He said his claims were based on interviews with others and that he could give details only in a classified setting. NASA and the Department of Defense denied that any such programmes exist, and no supporting evidence has been made public.",
 [('Cover-up','Supporters say the hearing showed that serious officials believe the government hides evidence of non-human craft, and they point to whistleblower laws passed by Congress. They say the lack of public evidence is due to secrecy.'),
  ('Second-hand claims','Sceptics and many scientists stress that Grusch did not see the craft himself and that independent experts have dismissed the claims. A 2024 report by the Pentagon\'s All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office found no evidence of recovered alien technology.')],
 ['wiki:David_Grusch_UFO_whistleblower_claims','wiki:All-domain_Anomaly_Resolution_Office'],img='File:David Grusch.jpg'),
X('calvine-1990','The Calvine photograph: a diamond-shaped UFO and a hovering jet',H,'disputed',3,1990,['photograph','official documents'],
 "On 4 August 1990 two men walking on moors near Calvine in Perthshire, Scotland, said they saw a large, silent, diamond-shaped object hovering above them, while a military jet, later identified as a Harrier, made low passes nearby. They gave photographs and negatives to the Daily Record newspaper, which passed them to the Ministry of Defence; the newspaper never ran the story and the negatives disappeared. Ministry of Defence files released in 2009 mentioned the case. In 2022 the journalist and researcher David Clarke tracked down a print kept by a former RAF press officer, Craig Lindsay, and it was published in August 2022. The witnesses have never been identified.",
 [('Best UFO photo?','Some ufologists and media called it one of the best UFO photographs ever taken. Nick Pope, who worked on UFO reports at the Ministry of Defence, said analysts thought it showed a real object.'),
  ('Reflection or hoax','Sceptics have suggested a reflection of a rock in water, a small object hung on a line, or a staged photo. A former head of the Pentagon\'s AARO, Sean Kirkpatrick, said he thought it showed a reflection and had been doctored.')],
 ['wiki:Calvine_UFO_photograph'],img=None),
X('red-sprites','Red sprites: lightning that goes up, doubted until 1989',H,'confirmed',2,1989,['photographic','eyewitness accounts','instrumental'],
 "Sprites are huge, brief flashes of red-orange light in the upper atmosphere, about 50–90 km high, above powerful thunderstorms. They are usually triggered by strong positive lightning strikes below and last only a few milliseconds. For a century, pilots and others reported strange lights shooting up from storm clouds; the earliest known report is from 1886, and the physicist C. T. R. Wilson predicted such discharges in 1925. Most scientists were sceptical until July 1989, when University of Minnesota researchers recorded one by accident with a low-light camera. Since then, sprites have been filmed thousands of times, including from the International Space Station.",
 [('Explained UFO reports?','Some researchers suggest that sprites and related flashes (blue jets and ELVES) may explain some reports of strange lights over storms. This is speculative for any particular case.'),
  ('Lesson about witnesses','Sprites are often cited as an example of reports that were dismissed for decades and then confirmed, which some use to argue that other unexplained sightings deserve study.')],
 ['wiki:Sprite_(lightning)','doi:10.1126/science.249.4964.48'],img='Sprite (lightning)',changed='1886: first known report; 1925: Wilson\'s prediction; 1989: first recorded on camera.'),
X('mpemba-effect','The Mpemba effect: a Tanzanian schoolboy and the hot water that freezes faster',H,'disputed',3,1963,['experimental'],
 "In 1963 Erasto Mpemba, a pupil at Magamba Secondary School in Tanganyika (now Tanzania), noticed in a cookery class that a hot ice-cream mixture froze before a cold one. His teachers and classmates laughed. Later, when the physicist Denis Osborne visited his school, Mpemba asked him about it. Osborne tested it, and in 1969 they published a joint paper. The idea that hot water can freeze faster than cold was mentioned long before by Aristotle, Francis Bacon and René Descartes. Today the 'Mpemba effect' has been studied in water, colloids and even quantum and magnetic systems.",
 [('It is real','Some experiments, and newer work on other systems, report a genuine effect under certain conditions, for example through evaporation, dissolved gases or convection.'),
  ('It depends on the setup','Other careful studies of water found no reproducible effect, and some argue it depends on how \'freezing\' is defined or on uncontrolled details. Physicists remain divided.')],
 ['wiki:Mpemba_effect','doi:10.1088/0031-9120/4/3/312'],img=None),
X('sailing-stones','The sailing stones of Death Valley: solved in 2014',H,'confirmed',3,2014,['observational','GPS tracking','time-lapse'],
 "On Racetrack Playa, a dry lake bed in Death Valley National Park, California, rocks weighing up to hundreds of kilograms leave long tracks across the flat mud, as if they had moved by themselves. The tracks were studied from the early 1900s, and suggested explanations included strong winds, ice, slime, and magnetism. Nobody had seen a stone move. In 2013–2014 a team led by Richard Norris and James Norris used GPS-tagged stones and time-lapse cameras and filmed the stones moving. Thin sheets of ice formed on a shallow pond, broke up on sunny days, and were pushed by light winds, sliding the stones a few metres per minute.",
 [('Paranormal ideas','Before 2014 the stones were cited in books on unexplained phenomena, with some suggesting magnetic or unknown forces. These ideas are now considered disproved.'),
  ('One answer for all?','Scientists note that tracks from different years may have formed in slightly different conditions, such as thicker ice or stronger winds.')],
 ['wiki:Sailing_stones','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0105948'],img='Sailing stones',dateNote='Tracks studied from the early 1900s; movement filmed 2013–2014'),
]
